## What Did NOT Work (and why)

- **`list_projects` via the Vercel MCP tool** — returned an empty array repeatedly even after the user confirmed the project existed (visible in their own dashboard at `vercel.com/marcusk639s-projects/veteran-claims-app`). Workaround: `get_project` with the exact project slug (`veteran-claims-app`) works fine and returns full details. Root cause unconfirmed — possibly a listing/pagination bug in that specific MCP tool, not a real access problem.
- **`deploy_to_vercel` MCP tool considered, then rejected** — it uploads a raw file tree with no git linkage. Using it would have either failed immediately (app throws via `getRequiredEnv` on missing secrets I don't have real values for) or succeeded in a worse state (a project disconnected from GitHub, breaking auto-deploy-on-push). Did not run it.
- **Public production routes initially all 302'd to `vercel.com/sso-api`** — because Vercel's default "Deployment Protection" (Vercel Authentication) was on, gating the entire deployment behind a Vercel team login regardless of the app's own auth logic. Fixed by user changing Project Settings → Deployment Protection → Vercel Authentication to "Only Preview Deployments."
- **`/dashboard` and `/dashboard/chat` return 404 in production instead of redirecting to `/sign-in`** — NOT fixed yet. Root cause confirmed via response headers: `x-clerk-auth-reason: protect-rewrite, dev-browser-missing`, and the HTML references `coherent-doe-38.clerk.accounts.dev` — the production env vars are using **Clerk development-instance keys** (the same ones used locally), not production `pk_live_...`/`sk_live_...` keys. Clerk's dev instances need a cross-origin "dev browser" JWT to resolve auth state; when it's missing (any fresh production visitor), newer Clerk middleware rewrites to a 404 instead of redirecting to sign-in, rather than the expected `auth.protect()` → `/sign-in` redirect.
- **Rebasing off a mid-work concurrent-session branch** — early in the session, `feat/founding-supporter-tier` was accidentally built on top of `docs/phase7-documentation`'s tip (another concurrent Claude session's in-progress, not-yet-pushed README/incident-response commit), discovered when `git branch --show-current` unexpectedly showed the wrong branch. Fixed via `git rebase --onto origin/main <old-parent> feat/founding-supporter-tier` to cleanly drop the dependency before merging to `main`. That other branch was later properly merged into `main` by the other session via a real merge commit (`02b0618`), so nothing was lost.
- **Git commands racing a concurrent session on the same shared working directory, again** — near session end, ran `git checkout -b docs/env-example-cron-and-gateway-key` → `git add .env.example` → `git commit` → `git push` to land the (by-then-fixed) `.env.example` change. `git commit` reported "nothing added to commit" — a concurrent session's own `git commit` for the identical fix had landed on whatever branch HEAD pointed to at that exact instant (this branch), under their own commit message, moments before mine ran. Their commit already had its own PR open (#9, from a differently-named branch `docs/env-example-cron-and-gateway-vars`, same commit hash). Recognized this via `git branch --contains <hash>` showing the same commit on two branch names, and `gh pr list` showing PR #9 already existed. Did not open a duplicate PR; deleted the redundant branch (local + remote) instead. **Lesson**: in this shared-working-directory setup, always check `gh pr list --state open` for an existing PR on the exact same file/topic before assuming a fresh branch+commit is actually new.

